-- THE [T-GLOBAL-TABLE] FIXTURE for the published evidence table (#2549 M2).
--
-- `evidenceRef` (compiler/types/route_key.mdk) is a program-global table keyed by an
-- `EvId`, and AGENTS.md's [T-GLOBAL-TABLE] names the required shape for a new one:
-- "the construct is present but the assertion is about code that never touches it".
-- Every other fixture in this corpus asserts a dispatch answer, so all of them read
-- the table; a table that answered the wrong goal to the RIGHT question would keep
-- them green. This fixture is the other half, and it is the only one here whose
-- printed values are chosen to be independent of dispatch.
--
-- The graph is deliberately full of the construct. cmod declares an interface and two
-- impls; wmod applies the constrained `cmod.tally` at two types, so two `EDictAt`
-- sites publish real slot-ordered routes under distinct ordinals; umod contributes an
-- unconstrained `pick` whose imported occurrence in wmod is the #739 arm, an `EDictAt`
-- with NO entry in the table at all (the miss that must stay silent). Ordinals for all
-- of those are minted from the same graph-global counter that numbers every other goal
-- in the run, including the bystander's.
--
-- main prints ONLY bystander.mdk's values. bystander imports nothing, declares no
-- interface, impls nothing and is monomorphic throughout, so no route lookup stands
-- between its source and its answer. If the table ever answers one goal's id with
-- another goal's routes -- the failure mode the module half of `EvId` is a tripwire
-- for, and which no "the feature works" fixture can see -- these two numbers are what
-- go wrong while every dispatch-asserting row in this file stays green.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER, hand-derived from the sources before any engine was run
-- ([WT-GOLDEN-ENSHRINES]):
--   bump (Tally 5)              = Tally 6
--   bump (Tally 6)              = Tally 7
--   bystanderValue              = 7
--   bystanderTotal              = 7 * 3 = 21
-- so: 7 then 21. Neither number contains a contribution from cmod, umod or wmod.
--
-- The dict-bearing bindings are forced but not printed, so the run genuinely executes
-- the dictionary path it elaborates. Their hand-derived values (208 and 3) are
-- recorded in wmod.mdk; they are NOT asserted here, on purpose.
--
-- The #739 site is carried by the VERDICT, not by a value: its symptom is a phantom
-- obligation recorded off the bare-name collision, which makes run and build
-- OVER-REJECT a valid program. This row pins ACCEPT on all three verbs, so that
-- regression reds it; a wrong-value regression at that site could not.
import wmod.{wValue, wPicked}
import bystander.{bystanderValue, bystanderTotal}

main =
  let _ = wValue
  let _ = wPicked
  let _ = println bystanderValue
  println bystanderTotal
